            <div class="">
              <p class="font-bold">Stage 5 The Caliphate and Mozarabic Route</p>
              <p class="">
                On your last cycling day, we follow two historic routes that
                lead us to the city of Córdoba, our final destination. The
                Caliphate Route is the Andalusian heritage that connects Córdoba
                and Granada, the capitals of the Caliphate and Nasrid
                al-Andalus. It was one of the busiest paths of the Iberian
                Peninsula in the Middle Ages, used by merchants from all over
                the known world, who supplied and traded with these important
                population centers; it was also the route of knowledge, science,
                and art.
              </p>
              <p class="">
                The other historic route is the Mozarabic Way to Santiago de
                Compostela. An ancient path that pays tribute to all Christians,
                the Mozarabs, who remained in the areas of Al-Andalus. A journey
                that reminds us of that time when these Christians, despite
                oppression, maintained their faith and devotion.
              </p>
              <p class="">
                The last part of the longest cycling route reveals our travel
                destination with views of the city of Córdoba, located in the
                Guadalquivir depression formed by the Guadalquivir River.
